---
/**
 * What it costs to leave.
 *
 * This section exists because the rest of the landing page argues the case for
 * adopting Luq and nothing on the site used to answer the question a reader
 * actually asks first: what happens when this turns out to be the wrong choice.
 *
 * Every claim below is a consequence of an arrangement documented elsewhere on
 * this site, not a promise made here:
 *
 *  - the type is `.for<T>()`'s argument, so nothing in the user's tree was
 *    authored by Luq and there is nothing to un-generate (Hero, README);
 *  - an undeclared path is not validated, not required and not read, so one
 *    `.v()` is a complete unit of both adoption and removal (Getting started);
 *  - the consumer's call site takes a Standard Schema, so the value in the slot
 *    can change without the call site changing (/standard-schema).
 *
 * The third card states the part that does not come back. A page that lists
 * only the cheap half of an exit is an advertisement, and the rest of this site
 * is built on publishing the losses next to the wins.
 */
const facts = [
  {
    icon: 'call_split',
    title: 'Enter one field at a time',
    body: 'A path you do not declare is not validated, not required and not read. One .v() is the whole unit: cover a single field on a single type, ship it, and leave the rest of the shape alone for as long as you like — a partly-covered type is a normal state rather than a half-finished migration.',
  },
  {
    icon: 'undo',
    title: 'Leave without touching your types',
    body: 'Your types were never authored here — .for<Order>() takes the Order you already had — so removing Luq deletes rules and imports, and nothing else.',
  },
  {
    icon: 'swap_horiz',
    title: 'The call site does not move',
    body: 'tRPC, react-hook-form, Hono and TanStack Form take a Standard Schema, and a Luq validator sits in the slot a zod schema would. Swapping the two changes the value passed in, not the code around it. What you would rewrite is the rules themselves — that part is real, and it is the same work in either direction.',
    href: '/standard-schema',
    hrefLabel: 'How the seam works',
  },
];
---

<section class="border-t border-outline-variant bg-surface px-4 py-14 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
  <div class="container mx-auto max-w-6xl">
    <div class="mb-8 text-center">
      <h2 class="mb-3 text-headline-medium text-on-surface">What it costs to leave</h2>
      <p class="mx-auto max-w-2xl text-body-large text-on-surface-variant">
        A choice you can undo in an afternoon does not need to be the right one on
        the first try.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-3">
      {
        facts.map((fact) => (
          <div class="md-card md-card-filled flex min-w-0 flex-col p-6">
            <div class="mb-4 flex h-10 w-10 items-center justify-center rounded-shape-md bg-primary-container">
              <span class="md-icon md-icon-sm text-on-primary-container" aria-hidden="true">
                {fact.icon}
              </span>
            </div>
            <p class="mb-2 text-title-medium text-on-surface">{fact.title}</p>
            <p class="text-body-medium text-on-surface-variant">{fact.body}</p>
            {fact.href && (
              <p class="mt-3 text-body-medium">
                <a class="text-primary underline underline-offset-2" href={fact.href}>
                  {fact.hrefLabel}
                </a>
              </p>
            )}
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
